{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5","id":"msg_011CdsZwrk3CUWzjHzw9zf1c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016wgpdwPBaWSdQiSHHTN4Gs","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-0/-home-user-creator-0/113f7cc5-237e-5d6a-8867-a97fd89c58dd/scratchpad/e6/e6-t11/primary/child-ws/search-report.md","content":"# Systematic search report: `add_days` in `inputs/dateadd.py`\n\n**Verdict: VIOLATION_FOUND.** The month-length table gives August 30 days\ninstead of 31 (`DAYS[7] = 30`). Every pair `(d, n)` in the domain whose\nday-by-day path strictly spans the true step August 30 → August 31 returns a\nresult exactly one calendar day ahead of the true Gregorian date. Smallest\nwitness: `add_days(\"2023-08-30\", 1)` returns `2023-09-01`; the true answer is\n`2023-08-31`.\n\n## 1. The code as written\n\n```python\nDAYS = [31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 30, 30, 31, 30, 31]\n\ndef _leap(y):\n    return y % 4 == 0 and (y % 100 != 0 or y % 400 == 0)\n\ndef _dim(y, m):\n    if m == 2 and _leap(y):\n        return 29\n    return DAYS[m - 1]\n\ndef add_days(iso, n):\n    y, m, d = (int(x) for x in iso.split(\"-\"))\n    for _ in range(n):\n        d += 1\n        if d > _dim(y, m):\n            d = 1\n            m += 1\n            if m > 12:\n                m = 1\n                y += 1\n    return f\"{y:04d}-{m:02d}-{d:02d}\"\n```\n\nThe loop applies a **successor function** `n` times: increment the day; if it\nexceeds `_dim(y, m)`, reset to day 1 of the next month, rolling December into\nJanuary of the next year. So `add_days(d, n)` is correct exactly when the\nsingle-step successor is correct at every date the traversal visits. That\nreduces the whole-domain audit to auditing:\n\n1. the `n = 0` identity (loop body never runs),\n2. `_leap` on the domain years,\n3. every entry of `DAYS` (via `_dim`) against the true month lengths,\n4. the `m > 12` year-rollover branch,\n5. propagation: whether a single wrong step can be compensated later.\n\n## 2. Table audit against the true Gregorian calendar\n\n| Index | Month | `DAYS` value | True length | Match? |\n|-------|-----------|--------------|-------------|--------|\n| 0 | January | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n| 1 | February | 28 | 28 (non-leap) | ✓ (leap handled by `_dim`) |\n| 2 | March | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n| 3 | April | 30 | 30 | ✓ |\n| 4 | May | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n| 5 | June | 30 | 30 | ✓ |\n| 6 | July | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n| **7** | **August** | **30** | **31** | **✗ — defect** |\n| 8 | September | 30 | 30 | ✓ |\n| 9 | October | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n| 10 | November | 30 | 30 | ✓ |\n| 11 | December | 31 | 31 | ✓ |\n\n`_leap`: 2023 → `2023 % 4 = 3` → False; 2024 → `2024 % 4 = 0`, `2024 % 100 =\n24 ≠ 0` → True; 2025 → `2025 % 4 = 1` → False. All three agree with the true\ncalendar (no century edge cases fall in 2023–2025 or within 60 days after).\n\n## 3. Class-by-class traces\n\n### 3.1 `n = 0` identity — correct\n`range(0)` is empty; the parsed `y, m, d` are re-formatted unchanged. For any\nvalid zero-padded ISO input the output equals the input. (Includes\n`add_days(\"2024-08-31\", 0) = \"2024-08-31\"` — the *input* Aug 31 round-trips;\nthe defect is only in *stepping onto* Aug 31.)\n\n### 3.2 In-month advances — correct\nWhile `d + 1 ≤ _dim(y, m)` the step is `d ← d + 1`, matching the true\nsuccessor regardless of the table (the boundary is not consulted below the\ntable value, and for August the code's limit 30 is *below* the true 31, so\nin-month steps up to day 30 are still true successors).\n\n### 3.3 The twelve month-boundary rollovers, `n = 1` from the last true day\n\n| Start | Code trace | Code result | True result | Match? |\n|-------|-----------|-------------|-------------|--------|\n| 2023-01-31 | 32 > 31 → roll | 2023-02-01 | 2023-02-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-02-28 | 29 > 28 → roll | 2023-03-01 | 2023-03-01 | ✓ |\n| 2024-02-28 | 29 ≤ 29 → stay | 2024-02-29 | 2024-02-29 | ✓ |\n| 2024-02-29 | 30 > 29 → roll | 2024-03-01 | 2024-03-01 | ✓ |\n| 2025-02-28 | 29 > 28 → roll | 2025-03-01 | 2025-03-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-03-31 | 32 > 31 → roll | 2023-04-01 | 2023-04-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-04-30 | 31 > 30 → roll | 2023-05-01 | 2023-05-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-05-31 | 32 > 31 → roll | 2023-06-01 | 2023-06-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-06-30 | 31 > 30 → roll | 2023-07-01 | 2023-07-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-07-31 | 32 > 31 → roll | 2023-08-01 | 2023-08-01 | ✓ |\n| **2023-08-30** | **31 > 30 → roll** | **2023-09-01** | **2023-08-31** | **✗** |\n| 2023-08-31 | 32 > 30 → roll | 2023-09-01 | 2023-09-01 | ✓ (coincidence) |\n| 2023-09-30 | 31 > 30 → roll | 2023-10-01 | 2023-10-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-10-31 | 32 > 31 → roll | 2023-11-01 | 2023-11-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-11-30 | 31 > 30 → roll | 2023-12-01 | 2023-12-01 | ✓ |\n| 2023-12-31 | 32 > 31, m=13 > 12 → y+1 | 2024-01-01 | 2024-01-01 | ✓ |\n\nThe August row fails identically in 2024 and 2025 (`_dim` takes the leap\nbranch only for February, so `_dim(y, 8) = DAYS[7] = 30` for every year):\n`add_days(\"2024-08-30\", 1) = \"2024-09-01\"` (true `2024-08-31`);\n`add_days(\"2025-08-30\", 1) = \"2025-09-01\"` (true `2025-08-31`).\nEvery non-August row was also confirmed for 2024 and 2025 — the traces are\nyear-independent except February (shown per-year above) and the December\nrollover, checked next.\n\n### 3.4 December 31 → January 1 for each domain year — correct\n- `add_days(\"2023-12-31\", 1)`: d=32 > 31 → d=1, m=13 > 12 → m=1, y=2024 → `2024-01-01`. True: `2024-01-01`. ✓\n- `add_days(\"2024-12-31\", 1)` → `2025-01-01`. True: `2025-01-01`. ✓\n- `add_days(\"2025-12-31\", 1)` → `2026-01-01`. True: `2026-01-01`. ✓ (start date in domain; result correctly leaves 2025.)\n\n### 3.5 Starts on the next-to-last true day, `n = 2` — correct except August\nFor every month where `DAYS` is right, next-to-last day + 2 = first of next\nmonth, matching truth (e.g. `2023-01-30 + 2 → 2023-02-01` ✓, `2024-02-28 + 2 →\n2024-03-01` ✓ via Feb 29). For August: `add_days(\"2023-08-29\", 2)`: step 1 →\nAug 30 (true: Aug 30 ✓); step 2 → 31 > 30 → `2023-09-01`, but true is\n`2023-08-31`. **✗ Violation.**\n\n### 3.6 Multi-month spans up to `n = 60`\nNot crossing the August defect — correct, e.g.:\n- `add_days(\"2023-01-31\", 60)`: Jan 31 →(1) Feb 1 →(29) Mar 1 →(59) Mar 31 →(60) Apr 1 = `2023-04-01`. True: 2023 Feb has 28 days, so Jan 31 + 60 = Apr 1. ✓\n- `add_days(\"2024-01-31\", 60)`: with Feb 29, lands `2024-03-31`; true `2024-03-31`. ✓\n\nCrossing the defect — violations:\n- `add_days(\"2023-07-15\", 60)`: code reaches Jul 31 at step 16, Aug 1 at 17,\n  Aug 30 at step 46, rolls to Sep 1 at step 47, ends `2023-09-14` at step 60.\n  True: Jul 31 at +16, Aug 31 at +47, `2023-09-13` at +60. **✗ off by +1 day.**\n- `add_days(\"2023-08-01\", 60)`: code: Aug 30 at step 29, Sep 1 at step 30,\n  Sep 30 at step 59, Oct 1 at step 60 → `2023-10-01`. True: Aug 31 at +30,\n  Sep 30 at +60 → `2023-09-30`. **✗ off by +1 day.**\n\n## 4. Characterization of the full violation set, and why it is exhaustive\n\nThe successor step is true-calendar-correct at every date except one: when the\ncode's state is **August 30** (any year), it rolls to September 1 instead of\nadvancing to the true August 31. After that single wrong step the code sits\nexactly one day ahead of the true date; since every subsequent month length it\nconsults (September onward, and any months after a December rollover) is\ncorrect, the one-day offset is preserved unchanged. With `n ≤ 60` no traversal\ncan reach a second August, so no double-counting or compensation is possible.\n\nTherefore the violating pairs are **exactly** those `(d, n)` in the domain\nwhose true interval strictly spans an August 30 → 31 boundary, i.e. start date\n`d ≤` August 30 of year `Y ∈ {2023, 2024, 2025}` and true end date\n`d + n ≥` August 31 of `Y`; on each such pair the code's output is the true\nanswer plus one day. All other pairs are correct, because every step they take\nis one of the verified-correct successor cases (in-month step below the\nmonth's true length, a rollover on a correct table entry, the leap-February\nbranch, or the December year rollover).\n\n## 5. Witnesses (all in-domain, smallest `n` first)\n\n| # | `(d, n)` | Code output | True output |\n|---|----------|-------------|-------------|\n| 1 | `(\"2023-08-30\", 1)` | 2023-09-01 | 2023-08-31 |\n| 2 | `(\"2024-08-30\", 1)` | 2024-09-01 | 2024-08-31 |\n| 3 | `(\"2025-08-30\", 1)` | 2025-09-01 | 2025-08-31 |\n| 4 | `(\"2023-08-29\", 2)` | 2023-09-01 | 2023-08-31 |\n| 5 | `(\"2023-07-15\", 60)` | 2023-09-14 | 2023-09-13 |\n| 6 | `(\"2023-08-01\", 60)` | 2023-10-01 | 2023-09-30 |\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6637,"cache_read_input_tokens":27545,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6637},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ceb7c822-f5ab-4653-9015-393f4ae9056e","uuid":"68dea8e4-c4a5-4cb7-9bc2-7e6fbe182bbb","timestamp":"2026-08-09T17:28:58.530Z","request_id":"req_011CdsZwq5aLYbtagmhom8gW"}
